1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,000
Parkinson's medication timing is set from a
ten-second finger-tapping test, scored by eye,

2
00:00:07,000 --> 00:00:14,000
once every few months. Five movement specialists
only agree with each other 74% of the time.

3
00:00:14,000 --> 00:00:22,000
Tapline runs that exact test on an iPhone,
entirely on-device. Prop your phone up, tap

4
00:00:22,000 --> 00:00:31,000
for ten seconds. Apple's Vision framework tracks
your thumb and index finger, frame by frame

5
00:00:31,000 --> 00:00:40,000
and Tapline measures what a clinician watches for:
how many taps, how fast, and how much smaller

6
00:00:40,000 --> 00:00:48,000
they get by the end.

7
00:00:48,000 --> 00:00:58,000
We didn't just build it, we checked it, on 234
real, clinician-scored patient videos.

8
00:00:58,000 --> 00:01:10,000
Where taps start shrinking, our adaptive detector
catches 8 taps on average. A fixed threshold catches 2.

9
00:01:10,000 --> 00:01:20,000
Adding amplitude and decrement to tap rate alone
improves the AUC from 0.58 to 0.68.

10
00:01:20,000 --> 00:01:28,000
This is Tapline Plus: a one-tap visit report and
history for a real neurologist visit.

11
00:01:28,000 --> 00:01:35,000
Tests stay free, forever. This is what's actually gated.

12
00:01:35,000 --> 00:01:43,000
And when Tapline can't measure reliably, it says
so, instead of guessing.

13
00:01:43,000 --> 00:01:50,000
Not a diagnosis. On-device. Open source.
